# Caddy — friendly hostnames instead of ports.
#
# Caddy reaches each service over the Compose network by service name and
# *container* port. That is deliberately not the same as the published host
# port: Sonarr publishes 127.0.0.1:8989 on the host, and Caddy talks to
# sonarr:8989 inside the network.
#
# WHAT IS PROXIED, AND WHAT IS NOT
#
# Only household services are proxied. Every admin service — the *arrs, SABnzbd,
# qBittorrent, Prowlarr, NZBHydra2, Bindery — is bound to loopback on purpose,
# and putting a hostname in front of one publishes a full-control surface, most
# with weak or disabled default authentication, to every device on the network.
# Stanzas for them are at the bottom, commented out, with what you are accepting
# written next to them.
#
# Set DOMAIN and CADDY_EMAIL in .env. With a real domain whose DNS points here,
# Caddy obtains genuine certificates automatically. Self-signed TLS is not
# offered: teaching a household to click through certificate warnings is a worse
# outcome than honest HTTP on a trusted LAN.

{
	email {$CADDY_EMAIL}
}

# ── household ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

watch.{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy jellyfin:8096
}

requests.{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy seerr:5055
}

books.{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy calibre-web-automated:8083
}

listen.{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy audiobookshelf:80
}

home.{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy homepage:3000
}

# ── admin — deliberately disabled ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Uncommenting any of these publishes an admin surface to the LAN. That is
# permitted, but it is a decision: the service's own authentication becomes the
# only thing between your network and full control of the stack. Put a Caddy
# `basic_auth` or forward-auth in front of it, and record why.
#
# sonarr.{$DOMAIN}      { reverse_proxy sonarr:8989 }
# radarr.{$DOMAIN}      { reverse_proxy radarr:7878 }
# lidarr.{$DOMAIN}      { reverse_proxy lidarr:8686 }
# bindery.{$DOMAIN}     { reverse_proxy bindery:8787 }
# bazarr.{$DOMAIN}      { reverse_proxy bazarr:6767 }
# prowlarr.{$DOMAIN}    { reverse_proxy prowlarr:9696 }
# nzbhydra.{$DOMAIN}    { reverse_proxy nzbhydra2:5076 }
# sabnzbd.{$DOMAIN}     { reverse_proxy sabnzbd:8080 }
# qbittorrent.{$DOMAIN} { reverse_proxy gluetun:8081 }
